window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Differential and Integral Calculus","h":"","i":"csc-114-differential-and-integral-calculus","x":"CSC 114 — Differential and Integral Calculus University of Embu · Mathematics and Statistics This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Differential and Integral Calculus","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Differential and Integral Calculus","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics 02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions 03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation 04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation 05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation 06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus 07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration 08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts 09 · Module 9 — Partial Fractions 10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Differential and Integral Calculus","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"","i":"csc-114-practical-exam-review","x":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review Use this as a last-pass method and formula map, not as a substitute for solving problems. Equations are written in plain text and Unicode for quick scanning."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"A reliable exam routine","i":"a-reliable-exam-routine","x":"A reliable exam routine Identify what is being asked: value, derivative, tangent, stationary point, antiderivative, definite accumulation, area, or approximation. Name the rule before using it. Show substitutions and intermediate algebra for method marks. Keep domains and units visible: denominators cannot be zero; logarithm inputs must be positive; trig-calculus formulas use radians. Check the result: differentiate an antiderivative, substitute into an equation, inspect signs, or verify the requested interval and units. If stuck, write the relevant definition/formula and make a valid first step rather than leaving the question blank."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"1–2 · Foundations","i":"12-foundations","x":"1–2 · Foundations Rational number: a/b, with integers a, b and b ≠ 0; reduce by common factors. Exponent laws: aᵐaⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (a ≠ 0). Radians: θ = s/r; degrees to radians: multiply by π/180. sin²x + cos²x = 1; sin(−x) = −sin x; cos(−x) = cos x. Complex z = a + bi: z̄ = a − bi; |z| = √(a² + b²); i² = −1. Line through two points: slope m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁); then y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"3–5 · Differentiation","i":"35-differentiation","x":"3–5 · Differentiation d/dx(c) = 0; d/dx(xⁿ) = n xⁿ⁻¹; differentiate sums term by term. Product: (fg)′ = f′g + fg′. Quotient: (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g². Chain: d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x))g′(x). Trig: (sin x)′ = cos x; (cos x)′ = −sin x; (tan x)′ = sec²x. Exponential/log: (eˣ)′ = eˣ; (aˣ)′ = aˣ ln a; (ln x)′ = 1/x. Stationary points: solve f′(x) = 0; f″ > 0 suggests minimum, f″ < 0 suggests maximum. If f″ = 0, test further. Tangent at x = a: y − f(a) = f′(a)(x − a). Newton–Raphson: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ); stop when estimates agree to the requested precision."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"6–10 · Integration","i":"610-integration","x":"6–10 · Integration ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C (n ≠ −1); ∫1/x dx = ln|x| + C. ∫eᵏˣ dx = eᵏˣ/k + C (k ≠ 0); always include + C for an indefinite integral. Definite integral: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a), where F′ = f. Area between curves: integrate upper − lower; split where curves cross. Accumulated rate: integrate rate over time. Displacement is ∫v; distance is ∫|v|. Substitution: choose u = inner function and replace du = u′ dx; consistently change or restore limits. Parts: ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du. Partial fractions: factor denominator, match each factor with a term, solve constants, then integrate. Integral average value: f_avg = (1/(b − a))∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx. The theorem guarantees f(c) = f_avg for some c in [a, b] if f is continuous."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"Method chooser","i":"method-chooser","x":"Method chooser What the integrand looks like First method to try A sum of powers or standard functions Integrate term by term A composite function times its inner derivative Substitution A product such as x eˣ or x sin x Integration by parts A proper rational function with a factorable denominator Partial fractions"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Practical Exam Review","h":"Final checks","i":"final-checks","x":"Final checks Did you use radians for trig derivatives/integrals? Is the denominator nonzero on the interval? Did you include + C only when appropriate? Did you evaluate upper limit minus lower limit? Is area positive, and did you split at crossings? Does the answer have the requested units and accuracy? For worked practice, use the 30-question bank and its worked answer key . Record weak topics and next actions in the progress tracker ."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Progress Tracker","h":"","i":"csc-114-exam-progress-tracker","x":"CSC 114 — Exam Progress Tracker After attempting the linked questions without notes, record your score out of 3, confidence, and a concrete next action. Use the linked topic note to repair gaps; retry missed questions after a delay. Module / topic Study note Question IDs Score (/3) Confidence (1–5) Review action 01 · Basic mathematics Open note M01-Q1 , M01-Q2 , M01-Q3 02 · Number systems and functions Open note M02-Q1 , M02-Q2 , M02-Q3 03 · Introduction to differentiation Open note M03-Q1 , M03-Q2 , M03-Q3 04 · Methods of differentiation Open note M04-Q1 , M04-Q2 , M04-Q3 05 · Applications of differentiation Open note M05-Q1 , M05-Q2 , M05-Q3 06 · Integral calculus Open note M06-Q1 , M06-Q2 , M06-Q3 07 · Applications of integration Open note M07-Q1 , M07-Q2 , M07-Q3 08 · Substitution and integration by parts Open note M08-Q1 , M08-Q2 , M08-Q3 09 · Partial fractions Open note M09-Q1 , M09-Q2 , M09-Q3 10 · Mean value theorem Open note M10-Q1 , M10-Q2 , M10-Q3"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Progress Tracker","h":"How to act on your result","i":"how-to-act-on-your-result","x":"How to act on your result 3/3 and confidence 4–5: do one delayed mixed-problem check, then maintain with brief recall. 2/3 or confidence 2–3: revisit the note section tied to the error, then retry the missed ID without notes. 0–1/3 or confidence 1: review the worked example, write the rule from memory, and redo all three questions. Write a specific action in the final column (for example, “redo M04-Q2; check quotient-rule sign on Friday”), not just “study more.”"},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"","i":"01-module-1-basic-mathematics","x":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics Source: Calculus Module 2. Use these number and algebra foundations before moving to functions and calculus."},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"Number sets","i":"number-sets","x":"Number sets Natural numbers: 0, 1, 2, 3, … (some conventions start at 1; follow the course convention). Integers: …, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, … Rational numbers: numbers expressible as a/b, where a and b are integers and b ≠ 0. A fraction is in lowest terms when numerator and denominator have no common factor except 1. Irrational numbers: real numbers that cannot be written as a ratio of integers, such as √2 and π. Every terminating decimal is rational: 1.4 = 14/10 = 7/5. A repeating decimal is rational too."},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"Operations and powers","i":"operations-and-powers","x":"Operations and powers Addition and multiplication are commutative and associative; multiplication distributes over addition: a + b = b + a; (a + b) + c = a + (b + c) ab = ba; (ab)c = a(bc) a(b + c) = ab + ac a + (−a) = 0; a × (−1) = −a For integer exponents (when the expressions are defined): aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ (ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ a⁰ = 1 for a ≠ 0; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ for a ≠ 0 Parity: an even integer has form 2k; an odd integer has form 2k + 1. Even + even and odd + odd are even; even + odd is odd."},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"Worked example: repeated doubling","i":"worked-example-repeated-doubling","x":"Worked example: repeated doubling A population starts at 300,000 and doubles every 20 years. From 1960 to 2020 there are three doubling intervals: Population = 300,000 × 2³ = 2,400,000."},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors Dividing by zero is undefined; a fraction’s denominator must be nonzero. (a + b)² is not a² + b²: expand it as a² + 2ab + b². A negative exponent means reciprocal, not a negative value: 2⁻³ = 1/8."},{"u":"notes/01_module-1-basic-mathematics.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall What distinguishes an integer, a rational number, and an irrational number? Is −15 odd? Write it in the form 2k + 1. Simplify 3² × 3⁴ and 2⁻² without a calculator. Check: −15 = 2(−8) + 1; 3² × 3⁴ = 3⁶ = 729; 2⁻² = 1/4. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"","i":"02-module-2-number-systems-and-functions","x":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions Source: Calculus Module 3. This module completes the course’s basic mathematics: functions, trigonometry, complex numbers, and analytic geometry."},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Functions","i":"functions","x":"Functions A function assigns exactly one output to every input in its domain. Write y = f(x); f(x) is the value at x. Functions on the same domain can be added or multiplied pointwise: (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x), (fg)(x) = f(x)g(x). A function is even if f(−x) = f(x), and odd if f(−x) = −f(x). For example, x² is even and x³ is odd."},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Trigonometry and radians","i":"trigonometry-and-radians","x":"Trigonometry and radians Radians measure angle by arc length: θ = s/r. On a unit circle, arc length s equals θ. Convert degrees to radians by multiplying by π/180; convert radians to degrees by multiplying by 180/π. Thus 180° = π radians and 90° = π/2. For a right triangle, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse and cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse. Essential identities: sin²x + cos²x = 1 sin(−x) = −sin x; cos(−x) = cos x sin(x + 2π) = sin x; cos(x + 2π) = cos x"},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Complex numbers","i":"complex-numbers","x":"Complex numbers A complex number is z = a + bi, where i² = −1. Add real and imaginary parts separately; multiply by expanding and replacing i² with −1. The conjugate of z = a + bi is z̄ = a − bi. z z̄ = a² + b² |z| = √(a² + b²), the distance from (a, b) to the origin Worked example: (2 + 3i)(1 − i) = 2 − 2i + 3i − 3i² = 5 + i."},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Analytic geometry: straight lines","i":"analytic-geometry-straight-lines","x":"Analytic geometry: straight lines The line y = mx + b has slope m and vertical intercept b. From two distinct points: m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), provided x₂ ≠ x₁. Through (x₁, y₁) with slope m, use point-slope form y − y₁ = m(x − x₁). Worked example: Through (1, 2) and (2, −1), m = (−1 − 2)/(2 − 1) = −3. Therefore y − 2 = −3(x − 1), or y = −3x + 5. To find a line–circle intersection, substitute the line equation into x² + y² = r², solve the resulting quadratic, then recover y."},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors A function cannot assign two different outputs to the same input. Degree and radian values are not interchangeable in calculus formulas; use radians. In complex multiplication, i² = −1, not 1. The slope formula fails for a vertical line (x₂ = x₁); such a line has equation x = constant."},{"u":"notes/02_module-2-number-systems-and-functions.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Is f(x) = x² + 1 even or odd? Why? Convert 150° to radians. Find the conjugate and modulus of 3 − 4i. Find the slope through (−1, 3) and (2, −6). Check: even; 5π/6; 3 + 4i and 5; slope −3. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"","i":"03-module-3-introduction-to-differentiation","x":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation Source: Calculus Module 4. Differentiation measures instantaneous change and gives the slope of a curve’s tangent."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Core rules","i":"core-rules","x":"Core rules For a constant c and a real power n: d/dx(c) = 0 d/dx(xⁿ) = n xⁿ⁻¹ d/dx(c f) = c f′ d/dx(f ± g) = f′ ± g′ Differentiate term by term after rewriting roots and reciprocals as powers, where defined. The derivative f′(x) gives the instantaneous rate of change at x; its sign indicates local increase (positive) or decrease (negative)."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Worked example: polynomial","i":"worked-example-polynomial","x":"Worked example: polynomial Let f(x) = 5x⁴ − 3x² + 7x − 6. f′(x) = 20x³ − 6x + 7. At x = 1, f′(1) = 20 − 6 + 7 = 21, so the instantaneous rate of change there is 21."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Worked example: tangent line","i":"worked-example-tangent-line","x":"Worked example: tangent line Find the tangent to f(x) = x² at x = 3. The derivative is f′(x) = 2x, so the tangent slope is 6. The point on the curve is (3, 9): y − 9 = 6(x − 3), so y = 6x − 9."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Practical interpretation","i":"practical-interpretation","x":"Practical interpretation If V(t) is a volume, then V′(t) > 0 means the volume is increasing at that instant; V′(t) < 0 means it is decreasing. Units also change: if V is in litres and t in minutes, V′ is in litres per minute."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors The power rule reduces the exponent by one: d/dx(x⁵) = 5x⁴, not 5x⁵. A constant term differentiates to zero. For a tangent line, use both the derivative (slope) and the function value (point). A negative derivative means decreasing locally; it does not by itself prove the function is negative."},{"u":"notes/03_module-3-introduction-to-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Differentiate 4x³ − 2x + 9. Find the tangent to y = x² + 1 at x = 2. What does a negative derivative say about a quantity’s instantaneous change? Check: 12x² − 2; y = 4x − 2; the quantity is decreasing at that instant. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"","i":"04-module-4-methods-of-differentiation","x":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation Source: Calculus Module 4. Identify the structure of an expression before choosing a rule."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Product, quotient, and chain rules","i":"product-quotient-and-chain-rules","x":"Product, quotient, and chain rules For differentiable f and g: Product: (fg)′ = f′g + fg′ Quotient: (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g², where g ≠ 0 Chain: if y = f(u) and u = g(x), then dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx) The chain rule handles a function inside another function: differentiate the outer function first, keep the inner expression, then multiply by the inner derivative."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Worked example: product","i":"worked-example-product","x":"Worked example: product Differentiate y = x² sin x. y′ = (2x)sin x + x² cos x."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Worked example: quotient","i":"worked-example-quotient","x":"Worked example: quotient For y = (x + 1)/(x − 1), x ≠ 1: y′ = [(1)(x − 1) − (x + 1)(1)]/(x − 1)² = −2/(x − 1)²."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Worked example: chain","i":"worked-example-chain","x":"Worked example: chain For y = (x² − 5)⁴, let u = x² − 5. Then dy/du = 4u³ and du/dx = 2x, so: dy/dx = 8x(x² − 5)³."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Choosing a method","i":"choosing-a-method","x":"Choosing a method A sum of terms? Use linearity and the basic rules on each term. A product? Use the product rule. A quotient? Use the quotient rule, or simplify first if possible. A nested expression? Use the chain rule. Products and chains may appear together."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors The derivative of a product is not f′g′. In the quotient rule, the numerator order is f′g − fg′; reversing it changes the sign. Do not forget the square on the denominator. For a chain, omitting the inner derivative loses a factor."},{"u":"notes/04_module-4-methods-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Differentiate y = (3x + 1)(x² − 2). Differentiate y = (2x − 1)³. State one way to check a quotient-rule result. Check: y′ = 9x² + 2x − 6; y′ = 6(2x − 1)²; differentiate the result independently or test with a numerical slope. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"","i":"05-module-5-applications-of-differentiation","x":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation Sources: Calculus Modules 5–6. This module covers standard derivative families, curve behaviour, extrema, and Newton–Raphson approximation."},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"Standard derivative families","i":"standard-derivative-families","x":"Standard derivative families Use the chain rule when the input is not simply x. Trigonometric: (sin x)′ = cos x; (cos x)′ = −sin x; (tan x)′ = sec²x Also: (cot x)′ = −csc²x; (sec x)′ = sec x tan x; (csc x)′ = −csc x cot x Exponential/logarithmic: (eˣ)′ = eˣ; (aˣ)′ = aˣ ln a; (ln x)′ = 1/x for x > 0 Inverse trig: (arcsin x)′ = 1/√(1 − x²); (arccos x)′ = −1/√(1 − x²); (arctan x)′ = 1/(1 + x²); (arccot x)′ = −1/(1 + x²) Also: (arcsec x)′ = 1/(|x|√(x² − 1)); (arccsc x)′ = −1/(|x|√(x² − 1)) Hyperbolic: (sinh x)′ = cosh x; (cosh x)′ = sinh x; (tanh x)′ = sech²x Also: (coth x)′ = −csch²x; (sech x)′ = −sech x tanh x; (csch x)′ = −csch x coth x Inverse hyperbolic: (arcsinh x)′ = 1/√(1 + x²); (arccosh x)′ = 1/√(x² − 1); (arctanh x)′ = 1/(1 − x²) Variable powers: for x > 0, (xʳ)′ = r xʳ⁻¹ and (xˣ)′ = xˣ(ln x + 1) For example, d/dx[sin(3x)] = 3 cos(3x); d/dx[eˣ²] = 2x eˣ²."},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"Stationary points and classification","i":"stationary-points-and-classification","x":"Stationary points and classification A stationary point satisfies f′(x) = 0. Find its coordinates by solving f′ = 0 and substituting each x into f. At a stationary point: f″(x) > 0: local minimum f″(x) < 0: local maximum f″(x) = 0: test inconclusive; inspect higher derivatives or the sign of f′ on each side. Worked example: f(x) = x² − 4x + 2. Then f′ = 2x − 4 = 0 gives x = 2; f(2) = −2. Since f″ = 2 > 0, (2, −2) is a minimum."},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"Newton–Raphson method","i":"newtonraphson-method","x":"Newton–Raphson method To approximate a root of f(x) = 0, start with a reasonable x₀ and iterate: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). Stop when consecutive values agree to the required accuracy and verify that f(xₙ) is small. The method can fail if f′(xₙ) = 0 or the starting value is poor. Worked example: For √10, solve f(x) = x² − 10 = 0. Then xₙ₊₁ = (xₙ + 10/xₙ)/2. Starting x₀ = 3 gives x₁ = 3.166667 and x₂ ≈ 3.162281, so √10 ≈ 3.1623."},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors In a composite function, multiply by the derivative of the inner expression. f″ = 0 alone does not prove a point of inflection or classify a stationary point. Newton–Raphson requires a nonzero derivative at each iterate; do not round too early."},{"u":"notes/05_module-5-applications-of-differentiation.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Differentiate y = ln(2x + 1). Classify the stationary point of y = −x² + 6x. Write one Newton step for f(x) = x³ − 2 starting at x₀ = 1. Check: 2/(2x + 1); a maximum at (3, 9), since y″ = −2; x₁ = 1 − (−1)/3 = 4/3. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"","i":"06-module-6-integral-calculus","x":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus Source: Calculus Module 7. Integration reverses differentiation; a definite integral accumulates signed change over an interval."},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Indefinite integrals","i":"indefinite-integrals","x":"Indefinite integrals An antiderivative F of f satisfies F′(x) = f(x). All antiderivatives differ by a constant: ∫ f(x) dx = F(x) + C. Core rules: ∫ k dx = kx + C ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C, for n ≠ −1 ∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + C ∫ eˣ dx = eˣ + C; ∫ eᵏˣ dx = eᵏˣ/k + C (k ≠ 0) Integrals distribute over sums and constant factors."},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Worked example: reverse the power rule","i":"worked-example-reverse-the-power-rule","x":"Worked example: reverse the power rule ∫(6x² − 4x + 3) dx = 2x³ − 2x² + 3x + C. Differentiate the answer to check: 6x² − 4x + 3."},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Definite integrals","i":"definite-integrals","x":"Definite integrals If F′ = f, the Fundamental Theorem gives: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a) = [F(x)]ₐᵇ. The integration constant cancels, so do not add + C to a definite-integral answer. Worked example: ∫₁³ 2x dx = [x²]₁³ = 9 − 1 = 8."},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Interpretation","i":"interpretation","x":"Interpretation A definite integral is signed accumulation: area above the axis contributes positively; area below contributes negatively. Units are “output units × input units.”"},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors For powers, divide by the new exponent n + 1; the formula excludes n = −1. Keep + C for indefinite integrals. Evaluate upper limit minus lower limit, not the reverse. ∫ₐᵇ f can be negative, even though geometric area is nonnegative."},{"u":"notes/06_module-6-integral-calculus.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Module 6 — Integral Calculus","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Find ∫(4x³ + 2/x) dx. Evaluate ∫₀² (3x² + 1) dx. Why is there no + C in a definite integral? Check: x⁴ + 2 ln|x| + C; [x³ + x]₀² = 10; the constants cancel when F(b) − F(a) is formed. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"","i":"07-module-7-applications-of-integration","x":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration Source: Calculus Module 8. Use integrals to measure area, accumulated quantities, and displacement."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Area and accumulation","i":"area-and-accumulation","x":"Area and accumulation If f(x) ≥ 0 on [a, b], the area under y = f(x) is ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx. If the curve crosses the axis, split at each crossing and add the absolute areas. For an upper curve f and lower curve g: Area = ∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx. First solve f(x) = g(x) to find intersection limits; if the curves swap order, split the interval."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Worked example: area under a curve","i":"worked-example-area-under-a-curve","x":"Worked example: area under a curve Find the area under y = x + 1 from x = 0 to x = 2. Since y is positive: Area = ∫₀² (x + 1) dx = [x²/2 + x]₀² = 4 square units."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Accumulated rate","i":"accumulated-rate","x":"Accumulated rate If R(t) is a rate in litres per minute, ∫ₐᵇ R(t) dt is the amount accumulated in litres. Worked example: Water flows at R(t) = 200 − 4t litres/minute. In the first 10 minutes: ∫₀¹⁰ (200 − 4t) dt = [200t − 2t²]₀¹⁰ = 1,800 litres."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Displacement and distance","i":"displacement-and-distance","x":"Displacement and distance For velocity v(t), displacement from a to b is ∫ₐᵇ v(t) dt. Net displacement may be negative. Total distance requires ∫ₐᵇ |v(t)| dt, so split where velocity changes sign."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors Area between curves is top minus bottom (or right minus left for horizontal slices), not simply either function. An integral of velocity gives signed displacement, not automatically total distance. Check intersections and axis crossings before integrating over the whole interval. Attach squared units to area and the proper accumulated units to rate integrals."},{"u":"notes/07_module-7-applications-of-integration.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Module 7 — Applications of Integration","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Find the area under y = 2x on [0, 3]. What does integrating a flow rate over time produce? When must a velocity integral be split to calculate total distance? Check: 9 square units; total accumulated volume; at every zero where velocity changes sign. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/08_module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts","h":"","i":"08-module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts","x":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts Source: Calculus Module 9. Choose substitution for a nested expression and integration by parts for a product that simplifies after differentiating one factor."},{"u":"notes/08_module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts","h":"Substitution","i":"substitution","x":"Substitution This reverses the chain rule. Set u equal to an inner expression and replace its differential consistently: u = g(x), du = g′(x) dx. If the integral is definite, either convert both limits to u-values or return to x before applying the original limits. Worked example: ∫2x cos(x²) dx. Set u = x², du = 2x dx: ∫cos u du = sin u + C = sin(x²) + C."},{"u":"notes/08_module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts","h":"Integration by parts","i":"integration-by-parts","x":"Integration by parts This reverses the product rule: ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du. Choose u so its derivative is simpler, and dv so it can be integrated. A useful heuristic is to choose a logarithm or algebraic factor for u. Worked example: ∫x sin x dx. Choose u = x, dv = sin x dx; then du = dx and v = −cos x: ∫x sin x dx = −x cos x + ∫cos x dx = −x cos x + sin x + C. For ∫x² sin x dx, apply parts twice: ∫x² sin x dx = −x² cos x + 2x sin x + 2 cos x + C."},{"u":"notes/08_module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors Substitution must replace both the expression and its differential; do not leave mixed x and u variables. With definite substitution, change both limits or change back to x—not one without the other. The parts formula has a minus sign: uv − ∫v du. Check by differentiating the final antiderivative."},{"u":"notes/08_module-8-integration-by-substitution-and-parts.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Module 8 — Integration by Substitution and Parts","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Evaluate ∫3x² eˣ³ dx. Choose u and dv for ∫x eˣ dx, then evaluate. What are the two consistent ways to handle definite-integral limits after substitution? Check: eˣ³ + C; u = x, dv = eˣ dx, giving eˣ(x − 1) + C; convert both limits to u or convert the antiderivative back to x before using x-limits. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"","i":"09-module-9-integration-by-partial-fractions","x":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions Source: Calculus Module 10. Decompose a rational function into simpler fractions, then integrate logarithmic forms."},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"When and how","i":"when-and-how","x":"When and how A rational function is a polynomial divided by a polynomial. If its numerator degree is at least the denominator degree, perform polynomial division first. Then: Factor the denominator completely. Write a partial-fraction form matching its factors. Clear denominators and solve for the unknown constants. Integrate each term; check by recombining or differentiating. For distinct linear factors (x − a)(x − b), write A/(x − a) + B/(x − b). For a repeated factor (x − a)², include A/(x − a) + B/(x − a)². Irreducible quadratics need a linear numerator."},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"Worked example: distinct factors","i":"worked-example-distinct-factors","x":"Worked example: distinct factors Decompose 1/[(x + 1)(x − 2)]: 1 = A(x − 2) + B(x + 1). Setting x = 2 gives B = 1/3; setting x = −1 gives A = −1/3. Therefore: 1/[(x + 1)(x − 2)] = −1/[3(x + 1)] + 1/[3(x − 2)]. So its integral is (1/3)ln|x − 2| − (1/3)ln|x + 1| + C."},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"Logarithm check","i":"logarithm-check","x":"Logarithm check ∫1/(x − a) dx = ln|x − a| + C. Absolute values are needed on intervals that do not cross the singularity."},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors Factor the denominator fully before choosing the decomposition. Include every power for repeated linear factors. Solve for constants carefully; substitution at roots is a quick check. Keep absolute values in logarithms, and exclude points where the denominator is zero."},{"u":"notes/09_module-9-partial-fractions.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · Module 9 — Integration by Partial Fractions","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall What is the correct form for A/[(x − 1)(x + 3)]? Decompose 1/[(x − 1)(x + 1)]. Why do we use ln|x| rather than only ln x when integrating 1/x? Check: A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 3); 1/2·1/(x − 1) − 1/2·1/(x + 1); the antiderivative is defined on either side of zero, with zero excluded. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"","i":"10-module-10-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","x":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals Source: Calculus Module 11. The theorem connects accumulated area with the average height of a continuous function."},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"The theorem and average value","i":"the-theorem-and-average-value","x":"The theorem and average value If f is continuous on [a, b], there is at least one c in [a, b] such that: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = f(c)(b − a). The average value is therefore: f_avg = (1/(b − a)) ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx. To find c, first calculate f_avg, then solve f(c) = f_avg and retain solutions in [a, b]. There may be more than one such c."},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"Worked example","i":"worked-example","x":"Worked example Find the average value of f(x) = 3x² − 2x on [1, 4], then find c. f_avg = (1/3)∫₁⁴(3x² − 2x)dx = (1/3)[x³ − x²]₁⁴ = 48/3 = 16. Set 3c² − 2c = 16. Then 3c² − 2c − 16 = 0 = (3c − 8)(c + 2), giving c = 8/3 or −2. Only c = 8/3 lies in [1, 4]."},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"Piecewise functions","i":"piecewise-functions","x":"Piecewise functions For a piecewise rate or speed, integrate separately on each segment and add the results. Divide by the total interval length to obtain the average value."},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"Common errors","i":"common-errors","x":"Common errors Divide by interval width b − a, not by the function’s endpoint values. Apply the theorem only when f is continuous on the closed interval. A solution to f(c) = f_avg outside [a, b] is not the required c. Average value is not generally f((a + b)/2)."},{"u":"notes/10_module-10-mean-value-theorem.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Find the average value of f(x) = 2x on [0, 3]. State the continuity condition for the integral mean value theorem. If f(c) = f_avg has two solutions, what do you do? Check: (1/3)∫₀³2x dx = 3; f must be continuous on [0, 3]; keep every solution in the interval. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"","i":"csc-114-exam-question-bank","x":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank Thirty targeted questions: three per course module. Attempt each one closed-book, show steps, and record your score and next action in the progress tracker . Worked solutions are in the answer key ."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","i":"module-1-basic-mathematics","x":"Module 1 — Basic Mathematics"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M01-Q1","i":"m01-q1","x":"M01-Q1 Simplify 3/4 − 5/8 and state why the result is rational."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M01-Q2","i":"m01-q2","x":"M01-Q2 Simplify (2x³)(−3x²) and evaluate 2⁻²."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M01-Q3","i":"m01-q3","x":"M01-Q3 A population of 300,000 doubles every 20 years. What is its population after 60 years?"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","i":"module-2-number-systems-and-functions","x":"Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M02-Q1","i":"m02-q1","x":"M02-Q1 Classify f(x) = x² − 2 as even, odd, or neither. Justify using f(−x)."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M02-Q2","i":"m02-q2","x":"M02-Q2 Convert 150° to radians and find sin(150°) and cos(150°)."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M02-Q3","i":"m02-q3","x":"M02-Q3 (a) Multiply (2 + 3i)(1 − i). (b) Find the equation of the line through (1, 2) and (2, −1)."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","i":"module-3-introduction-to-differentiation","x":"Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M03-Q1","i":"m03-q1","x":"M03-Q1 Differentiate f(x) = 5x⁴ − 3x² + 7x − 6 and find f′(1)."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M03-Q2","i":"m03-q2","x":"M03-Q2 Find the tangent line to y = x² + 1 at x = 2."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M03-Q3","i":"m03-q3","x":"M03-Q3 A tank’s volume is V(t) litres. At t = 5, V′(5) = −2.4. Interpret the sign and units if t is measured in minutes."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","i":"module-4-methods-of-differentiation","x":"Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M04-Q1","i":"m04-q1","x":"M04-Q1 Differentiate y = x² sin x."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M04-Q2","i":"m04-q2","x":"M04-Q2 Differentiate y = (x + 1)/(x − 1), stating the domain restriction."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M04-Q3","i":"m04-q3","x":"M04-Q3 Differentiate y = (3x² + 1)⁴."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","i":"module-5-applications-of-differentiation","x":"Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M05-Q1","i":"m05-q1","x":"M05-Q1 Differentiate y = ln(2x + 1) + sin(3x)."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M05-Q2","i":"m05-q2","x":"M05-Q2 Find and classify the stationary point of y = −x² + 6x."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M05-Q3","i":"m05-q3","x":"M05-Q3 Use one Newton–Raphson step from x₀ = 1 to approximate a root of f(x) = x³ − 2."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 6 — Integral Calculus","i":"module-6-integral-calculus","x":"Module 6 — Integral Calculus"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M06-Q1","i":"m06-q1","x":"M06-Q1 Find ∫(6x² − 4x + 3) dx and verify by differentiation."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M06-Q2","i":"m06-q2","x":"M06-Q2 Evaluate ∫₁³ 2x dx."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M06-Q3","i":"m06-q3","x":"M06-Q3 Evaluate ∫₀¹ e²ˣ dx."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 7 — Applications of Integration","i":"module-7-applications-of-integration","x":"Module 7 — Applications of Integration"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M07-Q1","i":"m07-q1","x":"M07-Q1 Find the area under y = x + 1 from x = 0 to x = 2."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M07-Q2","i":"m07-q2","x":"M07-Q2 A flow rate is R(t) = 200 − 4t litres/minute. Find the volume discharged from t = 0 to t = 10 minutes."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M07-Q3","i":"m07-q3","x":"M07-Q3 A particle has velocity v(t) = t − 2 m/s on [0, 4]. Find its displacement and total distance."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 8 — Substitution and Integration by Parts","i":"module-8-substitution-and-integration-by-parts","x":"Module 8 — Substitution and Integration by Parts"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M08-Q1","i":"m08-q1","x":"M08-Q1 Evaluate ∫3x² eˣ³ dx."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M08-Q2","i":"m08-q2","x":"M08-Q2 Evaluate ∫x eˣ dx using integration by parts."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M08-Q3","i":"m08-q3","x":"M08-Q3 Evaluate ∫₀¹ 2x cos(x²) dx using substitution. Show how the limits change."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 9 — Partial Fractions","i":"module-9-partial-fractions","x":"Module 9 — Partial Fractions"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M09-Q1","i":"m09-q1","x":"M09-Q1 Decompose 1/[(x − 1)(x + 1)] into partial fractions."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M09-Q2","i":"m09-q2","x":"M09-Q2 Evaluate ∫ dx/(x² − 1), where x ≠ ±1."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M09-Q3","i":"m09-q3","x":"M09-Q3 Write the correct partial-fraction form for (2x + 1)/[(x − 2)²(x + 3)] and state why all terms are needed."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","i":"module-10-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","x":"Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M10-Q1","i":"m10-q1","x":"M10-Q1 Find the average value of f(x) = 3x² − 2x on [1, 4], then find the value c guaranteed by the theorem."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M10-Q2","i":"m10-q2","x":"M10-Q2 Find the average value of f(x) = 2x on [0, 3]."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Exam Question Bank","h":"M10-Q3","i":"m10-q3","x":"M10-Q3 A continuous function has average value 5 on [2, 8]. State the integral it must satisfy and the equation used to find c."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"","i":"csc-114-worked-answer-key","x":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key Use these solutions to check reasoning, not just final values. If a method differs, verify by substitution, differentiation, or a numerical check."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 1 — Basic Mathematics","i":"module-1-basic-mathematics","x":"Module 1 — Basic Mathematics"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M01-Q1","i":"m01-q1","x":"M01-Q1 Use denominator 8: 3/4 − 5/8 = 6/8 − 5/8 = 1/8. It is rational because it is a ratio of integers with nonzero denominator."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M01-Q2","i":"m01-q2","x":"M01-Q2 Multiply coefficients and add exponents: (2x³)(−3x²) = −6x⁵. A negative exponent means reciprocal: 2⁻² = 1/2² = 1/4."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M01-Q3","i":"m01-q3","x":"M01-Q3 Sixty years contains 60/20 = 3 doubling periods. Population = 300,000 × 2³ = 2,400,000."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions","i":"module-2-number-systems-and-functions","x":"Module 2 — Number Systems and Functions"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M02-Q1","i":"m02-q1","x":"M02-Q1 f(−x) = (−x)² − 2 = x² − 2 = f(x), so f is even."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M02-Q2","i":"m02-q2","x":"M02-Q2 150° × π/180° = 5π/6. This is in quadrant II with reference angle π/6, so sin(150°) = 1/2 and cos(150°) = −√3/2."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M02-Q3","i":"m02-q3","x":"M02-Q3 (a) Expand and use i² = −1: (2 + 3i)(1 − i) = 2 − 2i + 3i − 3i² = 5 + i. (b) Slope m = (−1 − 2)/(2 − 1) = −3. Through (1, 2): y − 2 = −3(x − 1), hence y = −3x + 5."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation","i":"module-3-introduction-to-differentiation","x":"Module 3 — Introduction to Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M03-Q1","i":"m03-q1","x":"M03-Q1 By the power rule, f′(x) = 20x³ − 6x + 7. Thus f′(1) = 20 − 6 + 7 = 21."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M03-Q2","i":"m03-q2","x":"M03-Q2 y′ = 2x, so the slope at x = 2 is 4. The point is (2, 5). Point-slope form gives y − 5 = 4(x − 2), or y = 4x − 3."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M03-Q3","i":"m03-q3","x":"M03-Q3 V′(5) = −2.4 L/min means that at minute 5, the volume is decreasing instantaneously at 2.4 litres per minute."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation","i":"module-4-methods-of-differentiation","x":"Module 4 — Methods of Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M04-Q1","i":"m04-q1","x":"M04-Q1 Product rule: y′ = (2x)sin x + x² cos x."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M04-Q2","i":"m04-q2","x":"M04-Q2 For x ≠ 1, quotient rule gives y′ = [(1)(x − 1) − (x + 1)(1)]/(x − 1)² = −2/(x − 1)². The original function and derivative are undefined at x = 1."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M04-Q3","i":"m04-q3","x":"M04-Q3 Outer derivative times inner derivative: y′ = 4(3x² + 1)³(6x) = 24x(3x² + 1)³."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation","i":"module-5-applications-of-differentiation","x":"Module 5 — Applications of Differentiation"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M05-Q1","i":"m05-q1","x":"M05-Q1 Chain rule: d/dx ln(2x + 1) = 2/(2x + 1), and d/dx sin(3x) = 3 cos(3x). Therefore y′ = 2/(2x + 1) + 3 cos(3x)."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M05-Q2","i":"m05-q2","x":"M05-Q2 y′ = −2x + 6 = 0 gives x = 3; y(3) = 9. Since y″ = −2 < 0, the stationary point (3, 9) is a maximum."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M05-Q3","i":"m05-q3","x":"M05-Q3 Newton’s formula is x₁ = x₀ − f(x₀)/f′(x₀). Here f(1) = −1 and f′(x) = 3x², so f′(1) = 3. Thus x₁ = 1 − (−1)/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.3333."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 6 — Integral Calculus","i":"module-6-integral-calculus","x":"Module 6 — Integral Calculus"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M06-Q1","i":"m06-q1","x":"M06-Q1 Integrate term by term: ∫(6x² − 4x + 3) dx = 2x³ − 2x² + 3x + C. Differentiating gives 6x² − 4x + 3, as required."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M06-Q2","i":"m06-q2","x":"M06-Q2 An antiderivative of 2x is x². Therefore ∫₁³ 2x dx = [x²]₁³ = 9 − 1 = 8."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M06-Q3","i":"m06-q3","x":"M06-Q3 An antiderivative of e²ˣ is e²ˣ/2. Thus ∫₀¹ e²ˣ dx = [e²ˣ/2]₀¹ = (e² − 1)/2."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 7 — Applications of Integration","i":"module-7-applications-of-integration","x":"Module 7 — Applications of Integration"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M07-Q1","i":"m07-q1","x":"M07-Q1 The curve is above the axis, so area = ∫₀²(x + 1)dx = [x²/2 + x]₀² = 2 + 2 = 4 square units."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M07-Q2","i":"m07-q2","x":"M07-Q2 Volume = ∫₀¹⁰(200 − 4t)dt = [200t − 2t²]₀¹⁰ = 2,000 − 200 = 1,800 litres."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M07-Q3","i":"m07-q3","x":"M07-Q3 Displacement = ∫₀⁴(t − 2)dt = [t²/2 − 2t]₀⁴ = 0 m. Velocity changes sign at t = 2, so total distance is ∫₀²(2 − t)dt + ∫₂⁴(t − 2)dt = 2 + 2 = 4 m."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 8 — Substitution and Integration by Parts","i":"module-8-substitution-and-integration-by-parts","x":"Module 8 — Substitution and Integration by Parts"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M08-Q1","i":"m08-q1","x":"M08-Q1 Let u = x³, so du = 3x² dx. Then ∫3x²eˣ³ dx = ∫eᵘ du = eᵘ + C = eˣ³ + C."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M08-Q2","i":"m08-q2","x":"M08-Q2 Choose u = x and dv = eˣdx; then du = dx and v = eˣ. By parts, ∫xeˣdx = xeˣ − ∫eˣdx = eˣ(x − 1) + C."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M08-Q3","i":"m08-q3","x":"M08-Q3 Set u = x²; du = 2x dx. When x = 0, u = 0; when x = 1, u = 1. Therefore ∫₀¹2x cos(x²)dx = ∫₀¹cos u du = [sin u]₀¹ = sin 1."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 9 — Partial Fractions","i":"module-9-partial-fractions","x":"Module 9 — Partial Fractions"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M09-Q1","i":"m09-q1","x":"M09-Q1 Set 1/[(x − 1)(x + 1)] = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 1). Clearing denominators gives 1 = A(x + 1) + B(x − 1). At x = 1, A = 1/2; at x = −1, B = −1/2. Thus the decomposition is 1/[2(x − 1)] − 1/[2(x + 1)]."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M09-Q2","i":"m09-q2","x":"M09-Q2 Using M09-Q1, integrate term by term: ∫dx/(x² − 1) = (1/2)ln|x − 1| − (1/2)ln|x + 1| + C."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M09-Q3","i":"m09-q3","x":"M09-Q3 The denominator has a repeated linear factor and a distinct linear factor, so include each power of x − 2 and the x + 3 term: (2x + 1)/[(x − 2)²(x + 3)] = A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2)² + C/(x + 3). All three terms are needed to represent a general numerator over that denominator."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","i":"module-10-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals","x":"Module 10 — Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M10-Q1","i":"m10-q1","x":"M10-Q1 Average value = (1/(4 − 1))∫₁⁴(3x² − 2x)dx = (1/3)[x³ − x²]₁⁴ = 48/3 = 16. Solve 3c² − 2c = 16: (3c − 8)(c + 2) = 0, so c = 8/3 or −2. Only c = 8/3 lies in [1, 4]."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M10-Q2","i":"m10-q2","x":"M10-Q2 Average value = (1/3)∫₀³2x dx = (1/3)[x²]₀³ = 9/3 = 3."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 114 — Worked Answer Key","h":"M10-Q3","i":"m10-q3","x":"M10-Q3 The interval width is 8 − 2 = 6, so continuity and the theorem give ∫₂⁸ f(x)dx = 5 × 6 = 30. To find c, solve f(c) = 5 and retain a solution in [2, 8]."}];